\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Radiation-enhanced antennas, often termed "beamforming antennas," utilize the wave propagation characteristics of the radiation pattern to achieve improved performance in terms of gain, beamwidth, and directionality. Unlike traditional isotropic antennas, which radiate equally in all directions, radiation-enhanced antennas utilize directional characteristics to focus power where it is needed. This makes them particularly useful in scenarios where monostatic coverage is insufficient, such as in free space, indoor environments, or high-frequency bands where direct line-of-sight is unreliable.
\label{sec:radiation}

\section{Design and Implementation}
\label{sec:design}
The design of radiation-enhanced antennas typically involves decoupling the radiation pattern from the physical antenna structure. This is often achieved through a dielectric substrate or metallic structure to increase electric field density at the aperture. The choice of materials influences the radiation efficiency; for instance, using a dielectric layer near the feed point can reduce loss compared to a pure metal structure. The configuration is often optimized to minimize insertion loss while maximizing gain, often through the use of multiple elements.

